    "slug": "how-to-host-a-multilingual-webinar",
    "title": "How to host a multilingual webinar with live translation",
    "excerpt": "Broadcast to an international audience without booking interpreters: what a live-translated webinar is, how to set one up, and exactly what your attendees see and hear in their own language.",
    "content": "<p>A webinar reaches people who do not share your language. Usually that means one of three compromises: run the session in English and lose the room, repeat it once per region, or hire simultaneous interpreters. A live-translated webinar removes the choice — you present once, in your own language, and every attendee follows along in theirs.</p>\n\n<h2>A webinar is not a meeting</h2>\n<p>The distinction matters, because the two work differently. A VoxTranslate <strong>meeting</strong> is a small peer-to-peer call: everyone speaks, everyone is translated, up to four people. A <strong>webinar</strong> is a broadcast: one host presents to an audience of any size, and the audience watches rather than talks. Because it is one-to-many, the audience needs no camera, no microphone, and no account — just a link.</p>\n\n<h2>Before you go live</h2>\n<h3>Pick the source language, not the audience's</h3>\n<p>You set the language <em>you</em> will speak. Translation flows outward from there, so present in the language you are most fluent and most persuasive in. Your accent, your idioms and your pace are all easier to work with when you are not translating in your head.</p>\n\n<h3>Decide who can join</h3>\n<p>Every webinar gets a short join link and a QR code. A private webinar is reachable only by that link — good for customer training or an internal all-hands. A public one is also listed in the app, which is what you want for open sessions you are actively promoting. If the session should be restricted to signed-in members, that too is a switch, not a workaround.</p>\n\n<h3>Schedule it, or start now</h3>\n<p>You can open a webinar immediately or schedule it, which gives you a calendar entry to share and reminders for attendees. For anything you are promoting in advance, schedule it: the link stays stable, so it can go into the invite, the newsletter and the landing page long before you go live.</p>\n\n<h2>Going live</h2>\n<p>As host you publish your microphone, and optionally your camera. That is the whole setup — there is no software to install for you or for your audience, because the broadcast runs in the browser. Speech recognition transcribes you as you talk, and the transcript is translated in parallel into every language present in the room, so a session with attendees across five languages is handled at once rather than five times.</p>\n\n<h2>What your audience actually gets</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Live subtitles in their own language</strong>, chosen per person — nobody has to settle for a shared second language.</li>\n  <li><strong>Spoken translation</strong>, so attendees can listen instead of reading a transcript while also watching your slides.</li>\n  <li><strong>Translated chat.</strong> A question typed in Portuguese reaches you in your language, and your answer reaches the room in theirs. This is where multilingual webinars usually break down, and it is the part most worth having.</li>\n  <li><strong>No account, no download.</strong> Attendees open the link, pick a display name, and they are in. Every extra step between an invite and a live face costs you attendance.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Five things worth doing</h2>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Speak in complete sentences.</strong> Recognition and translation both work on clause boundaries; trailing off mid-thought is the main cause of an awkward subtitle.</li>\n  <li><strong>Say product names deliberately.</strong> Proper nouns are the hardest thing for any transcription system. Slow down half a beat on yours.</li>\n  <li><strong>Pause after questions.</strong> Translation and speech take a moment to arrive. A two-second pause is the difference between a conversation and people talking over the translation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Put the link and QR code on your last slide.</strong> Attendees who arrive late or want to share it with a colleague will look for it there.</li>\n  <li><strong>Enable the transcript</strong> if you plan a recap. A multilingual transcript is a follow-up email, a blog post and a searchable record for the people who could not attend.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>Where this pays off</h2>\n<p>The pattern is the same for a product launch, a partner briefing, a customer training session or a company all-hands: an audience that spans languages, a message you only want to deliver once, and questions you actually want to receive — including from the people whose English would have kept them silent.</p>\n\n<h2>A note on AI output</h2>\n<p>Transcription and translation are produced by AI systems and can contain mistakes, and the spoken translation attendees hear is computer-generated rather than a recording of your voice. For everyday business communication that trade-off is easily worth it; for legally binding or safety-critical proceedings, a human interpreter is still the right call.</p>\n",
